</style></head> <body> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Organization","@id":"https://aialleyway.com/#organization","name":"AI Alleyway","url":"https://aialleyway.com","description":"Honest deep dives on the AI tools worth paying for — and the ones that aren't.","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://aialleyway.com/brand/logo-512.jpg","width":512,"height":512},"sameAs":["https://x.com/aialleywaycom","https://www.linkedin.com/company/ai-alleyway","https://www.facebook.com/people/AI-Alleyway/61584530824827/","https://github.com/ai-alleyway","https://www.crunchbase.com/organization/ai-alleyway","https://youtube.com/@ai.alleyway"]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://aialleyway.com/#website","url":"https://aialleyway.com","name":"AI Alleyway","alternateName":"aialleyway.com","publisher":{"@id":"https://aialleyway.com/#organization"}}</script> <script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://aialleyway.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Build","item":"https://aialleyway.com/category/build/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Coding","item":"https://aialleyway.com/category/build/coding/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":4,"name":"Cursor review: I paid for 11 months, then cancelled","item":"https://aialleyway.com/cursor-review/"}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Product","name":"Cursor","description":"Cursor review from someone who actually paid: $510.70 across 11 months, a third of it metered usage. What it does well, and what I got wrong about the bill.","url":"https://aialleyway.com/cursor-review/","image":"https://aialleyway.com/og/cursor-review.webp","brand":{"@type":"Brand","name":"Cursor"},"review":{"@type":"Review","author":{"@type":"Person","name":"Ryan Parker","url":"https://aialleyway.com/author/ryan-parker/","knowsAbout":["Voice and audio AI","AI music generation","Presentation and slide AI","AI coding assistants","No-code and app builders","Workflow automation"]},"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"AI Alleyway"},"datePublished":"2026-07-28T00:00:00.000Z","dateModified":"2026-08-01T00:00:00.000Z","reviewBody":"Cursor is an AI-native code editor built on VS Code, now reshaped around an agent-first home screen rather than the tab-completion editor most reviews still describe. I score it 4.2 out of 5, a Power Tool. I paid for it for eleven months, from March 2025 to January 2026, then cancelled to a free plan. The editor is good, the model range is useful, and for five straight months it did the job at a flat $20. The catch is metering: usage beyond your included allowance is billed in arrears at the model's API rate, and across my eleven months that came to $170.70 of a $510.70 total. Two months cleared $100. Checking Cursor's docs while writing this corrected me on three points worth stating: the Pro Plus and Ultra prices are published, individual plans pay API rates with no Cursor markup, and a monthly spend limit is available on Pro, Pro+ and Ultra. I never set one. Buy it if you code moderately, or if you will cap your spend on day one. Look elsewhere if you want a flat all-in price at heavy volume.","reviewRating":{"@type":"Rating","ratingValue":4.2,"bestRating":5,"worstRating":1,"name":"Power Tool"}}}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Cursor worth it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"It depends entirely on how hard you push it, which is not the answer the pricing page implies. I paid for Cursor for eleven months and it was good value for five of them, when my bill was a flat $20 and the included allowance covered everything I did. The editor is capable, the model range is wide, and building on VS Code means your extensions and shortcuts come with you.\n\nIt stopped being worth it for me when I started running agents seriously. Usage past your included allowance is billed after the fact, and my October 2025 bill came to $159.22 with roughly half of that unplanned.\n\nAcross the full eleven months I paid $510.70, of which $170.70 was on-demand billing rather than subscription. If you code moderately, it is a solid $20 tool. If you delegate heavily, price the meter rather than the plan. I score it 4.2 out of 5."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much does Cursor actually cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Hobby is free with no card required. The paid individual tiers are Pro at $20 a month, Pro Plus at $60 and Ultra at $200, and teams pay $40 or $120 per user. Only the $20 shows on the pricing card itself, because Pro Plus and Ultra sit behind a tier picker, but all three prices are published in Cursor's billing docs.\n\nThose are not the numbers on your card if you use agents at volume. Every plan includes a set amount of model usage, and on-demand usage continues past it, billed in arrears at the model's API rate.\n\nIn my case that added $170.70 across eleven months on top of $340 in subscriptions. Prices also exclude tax. You can cap the metered part with a monthly spend limit from the Spending tab, on any paid individual plan, which is the single most useful thing to know before subscribing."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did you switch from Cursor to Claude Code?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Cost predictability, not capability. When I upgraded to the $60 plan the included allowance ran out in about a week, and metered charges pushed my monthly total past $100 more than once. I had assumed Cursor was taking a margin on the Anthropic models my invoices show me calling, and while writing this review I checked and found that is wrong: individual plans are charged at the model's API price, with no Cursor markup.\n\nSo the honest reason is arithmetic rather than grievance. Metered API pricing is fair and it is also unbounded, and at my volume a capped subscription won. My two heaviest Cursor months totalled $159.22 and $110.00, of which $79.22 and $50.00 were metered; Claude Max 5x bills me $110 including tax, with a hard cap.\n\nI should add that Cursor offers a spend limit on paid individual plans and I never set one, which would have changed the picture. I cancelled and was downgraded to the free tier, where the account still sits."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Cursor still just a VS Code fork?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Less than it used to be, and this is where most reviews are out of date. Cursor started as a VS Code fork with AI woven into the editor, and most published reviews still describe it that way, leading on tab completion and inline edits.\n\nOpen it today and the default screen is an agent prompt reading 'Plan, Build, / for skills, @ for context', with a Composer model selector and a Plan New Idea button. There are skills, hooks, a /shell command and a plan mode.\n\nThe VS Code lineage is still there and your extensions still work, but the product's centre of gravity has moved from typing alongside an assistant to briefing an agent, which puts it much closer to terminal agents than the older reviews suggest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Cursor better than Claude Code?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For most people who want AI inside an editor, yes. Cursor keeps you in a VS Code window with your extensions and keybindings intact, shows you changes inline, and starts at $20 against Claude Max's $100. If you want to watch the work happen rather than review it afterwards, Cursor is the better fit and the cheaper one.\n\nI moved to Claude Code anyway, for one reason: my Cursor bill was metered and unbounded, and at my volume a capped plan won. That is a statement about my usage, not about which tool writes better code. I scored the two head to head in Claude Code vs Cursor, where three axes tie and Cursor takes three of the rest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is Cursor's free plan good for?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Evaluating the tool, and not much beyond that. The Hobby tier costs nothing, needs no credit card, and gives you limited agent requests plus access to Composer, which is enough to judge whether the workflow suits you before paying anything.\n\nThe limits bite quickly on real work, and the better models carry a lock icon until you upgrade, so you can see what you are missing without being able to use it. Treat it as a genuine trial rather than a permanent home.\n\nThat said, it is a more honest free tier than most, because you reach the actual product rather than a crippled demo, and my own cancelled account has sat on it since January without being nagged into resubscribing. If you are trying to work out whether you would blow past the included allowance on a paid plan, a fortnight of real work on Hobby will answer that faster than any pricing page can."}}]}</script> <header class="site-header brand-surface" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> <div class="site-header__inner" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> <!-- Brand mark --><a href="/" class="site-header__brand" aria-label="AI Alleyway — home" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>  <img src="/brand/logo-88.webp" alt="AI Alleyway" class="site-header__brand-logo" width="44" height="44" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> </a> <!-- Nav + toggle --> <nav class="site-header__nav" aria-label="Main navigation" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> <ul class="site-header__nav-list" role="list" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> <li data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs> <a href="/blog/" class="site-header__nav-link" data-astro-cid-nen7h5rs>
